%S A399681 1,2,4,3,6,5,10,7,14,9,12,11,22,13,26,15,18,17,34,19,38,21,24,23,46,
%T A399681 25,20,8,16,28,30,27,33,36,29,58,31,62,35,40,32,42,39,45,37,74,41,82,
%U A399681 43,86,47,94,49,56,51,48,44,50,52,54,57,60,55,65,53,106,59,118,61
%N A399681 a(1) = 1, a(2) = 2; for n > 2, a(n) is the smallest unused positive number that shares a factor with a(n-1) if a(n-1) <= n-1, else is coprime to a(n-1) if a(n-1) > n-1.
%C A399681 The sequence contains large groups of composite terms less than n which are terminated when a term a(n) becomes greater than n. This is followed by a series of primes p less than n alternating with terms 2*p that are greater than n. This continues until a composite a(n) < n can be chosen that is coprime to the previous term, after which the above pattern repeats. This leads to the lower line in the graph of the terms having a similar appearance to that of A375564. See the attached image.
%C A399681 For the terms studied the fixed points are 1, 2, 33, 40, 42, 112, 663, 671, 962. It is plausible no more exist. The sequence is conjectured to be a permutation of the positive integers.
%C A399681 The sequence shares the first twenty-seven terms with A091857.
%H A399681 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A399681/b399681.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399681 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A399681/a399681_1.png">Plot prime(i)^m | a(n) at (x,y) = (n,i)</a> for n = 1..2048, 4X vertical exaggeration, with a color function showing m = 1 in black, m = 2 in red, m = 3 in orange, ..., m = 10 in magenta. Under the plot, the index of the band of colors indicates the term a(n) is prime (red), proper prime power (gold), squarefree composite (green), or neither squarefree nor prime power (blue).
%H A399681 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A399681/a399681.png">Image of the first 100000 terms</a>. The prime values are shown in red, composites in white. The thin green line is a(n) = n.
%e A399681 a(5) = 6 as a(4) = 3, and as 3 <= 4, a(5) is the smallest unused number that shares a factor with 3, being 6.
%e A399681 a(8) = 7 as a(7) = 10, and as 10 > 7, a(8) is the smallest unused number that is coprime to 10, being 7.
%t A399681 nn = 120; c[_] = False; j = 2; u = 3; s = {1, 2}; Map[Set[c[#], True] &, s];
%t A399681 {1, 2}~Join~Reap[Do[k = u; If[j < n, While[Or[c[k], CoprimeQ[j, k]], k++], While[Or[c[k], ! CoprimeQ[j, k]], k++] ]; Sow[k]; Set[{c[k], j}, {True, k}]; If[k == u, While[c[u], u++] ], {n, 3, nn}] ][[-1, 1]] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Oct 21 2026 *)
